Netflix is the way we watch TV, but some fear that Netflix could be closing the curtains on the big screen.

872
01:33:54,644 --> 01:34:01,644
So, is there any evidence to suggest that our love for streaming movies at home could spell the end for cinema?

873
01:34:01,644 --> 01:34:06,644
US-based company Netflix has had more than a few reasons to celebrate recently.

874
01:34:06,644 --> 01:34:15,644
It's added a record-breaking number of paid users to its online streaming service in 2018, now topping 139 million worldwide.

875
01:34:15,644 --> 01:34:18,644
That's three times the number of accounts just five years ago.

876
01:34:18,644 --> 01:34:24,644
Keep in mind that more than one person could be using an account, so its reach is even bigger.

877
01:34:24,644 --> 01:34:31,644
The USA is Netflix's top market globally, with over 40% of all paid users based there.

878
01:34:31,644 --> 01:34:37,644
The company has also invested heavily into producing original content, including big movie blockbusters.

879
01:34:37,644 --> 01:34:44,644
Analysts estimate that the company spent $13 billion on titles like Narcos and Bird Box in 2018.

880
01:34:44,644 --> 01:34:51,644
That would be enough to make Titanic, one of the most expensive films ever made 65 times.

881
01:34:51,644 --> 01:34:53,644
Its efforts seem to be paying off.

882
01:34:53,644 --> 01:35:02,644
This year, its critically acclaimed film Roma has earned a streaming service, its first Best Picture Oscar nomination, with 10 in total.

883
01:35:02,644 --> 01:35:11,644
You might think that can only benefit the box office, but Netflix has been criticised for showing films in a relatively small number of movie theatres,

884
01:35:11,644 --> 01:35:16,644
and for a limited time before streaming them on its own site.

885
01:35:16,644 --> 01:35:20,644
So, are there any signs that cinemas are suffering?

886
01:35:20,644 --> 01:35:21,644
Not really.

887
01:35:21,644 --> 01:35:28,644
While analysts said that 2017 was a tough year for movie makers, last year saw a boost for the box office.

888
01:35:28,644 --> 01:35:32,644
Worldwide, cinemas made over $41.5 billion.

889
01:35:32,644 --> 01:35:37,644
That's 2.6% more than in 2017.

890
01:35:37,644 --> 01:35:43,644
The top three markets, the USA, China and the UK, also increased in revenues too.

891
01:35:43,644 --> 01:35:48,644
And, moviegoers in America generated nearly $12 billion for the industry.

892
01:35:48,644 --> 01:35:57,644
There are other reasons to be sceptical of the suggestions that the rising popularity of Netflix is to blame for the perceived decline of cinema.

893
01:35:57,644 --> 01:36:04,644
Comparing the trends in cinema revenues and streaming services tells us little about how one impacts the other.

894
01:36:04,644 --> 01:36:12,644
Just like the consumption of mozzarella cheese can't really explain the rise in the number of civil engineering doctorates awarded,

895
01:36:12,644 --> 01:36:15,644
even though they're both going up.

896
01:36:15,644 --> 01:36:24,644
There are a number of other factors at play which are likely to contribute to the possible success or failure of cinemas and streaming services.

897
01:36:24,644 --> 01:36:32,644
But, is there any evidence to suggest that Netflix and movie theatres are competing for audiences?

898
01:36:32,644 --> 01:36:34,644
That's a tricky one too.

899
01:36:34,644 --> 01:36:46,644
One US study by NATO, that's the National Association of Theatre Owners in the States, not the North Atlantic Treaty Organisation, surveyed just under 2,000 people in the US,

900
01:36:46,644 --> 01:36:51,644
who both streamed content and saw at least one film in the cinema over the last year.

901
01:36:51,644 --> 01:37:01,644
They found that people who watch a lot of stream programmes also go to the cinema frequently, while those who don't subscribe rarely visit the movie theatre either.

902
01:37:01,644 --> 01:37:08,644
But even that doesn't tell us conclusively about how the use of one platform actually affects people's cinema habits.

